1a) AI vs ML
AI is the bigger concept. It means making computers perform tasks that normally require human intelligence.
Examples:
Understanding language
Recognizing images
Playing games
Making decisions
Generating text or images
Driving autonomously
ML: Instead of explicitly programming every rule, we give the computer data and examples, and it learns patterns from that data.
Artificial Intelligence (AI)
│
├── Machine Learning (ML)
│   ├── Supervised Learning
│   ├── Unsupervised Learning
│   └── Reinforcement Learning
│
├── Deep Learning (DL)
│
├── Natural Language Processing (NLP)
│
├── Computer Vision
│
└── Robotics
Real life example:
Suppose you want a computer to identify cats.
Traditional AI approach:
"If the image has two eyes, two ears, fur, and a tail → CAT."
This becomes difficult because cats can look very different.
ML approach:
Give the computer thousands of cat and non-cat images → let it learn the patterns.

1b) Machine Learning vs Deep Learning
Machine Learning
Machine Learning uses algorithms to learn patterns from data.
Examples:
Linear Regression
Logistic Regression
Decision Tree
Random Forest
K-Means
SVM
XGBoost
For many ML algorithms, humans need to decide which features are important.

Deep Learning
Deep Learning is a subset of Machine Learning that mainly uses neural networks with many layers.
Examples:
Neural Networks
CNN
RNN
LSTM
Transformer
Deep learning can automatically learn useful features from large amounts of raw data.
example:
Image
  ↓
Neural Network
  ↓
Features learned automatically
  ↓
Cat / Dog
Easy analogy
Imagine teaching a child to recognize a dog.
Traditional ML:
You help the child notice:
"Look at the ears, size, legs, tail, etc."
Deep Learning:
You show the child thousands of examples, and the system learns useful visual patterns by itself.
